# Roadmap

Current state of COSS for Svelte and where the upstream COSS project is headed next.

<CopyMarkdownButton url="/docs/roadmap.md" />

**COSS UI** is the evolution of **Origin UI**, an open-source collection of React and Tailwind
components created by Pasquale Vitiello and Davide Pacilio. It was acquired by Cal.com and developed
into a broader ecosystem, with Pasquale contributing directly to the new UI.

The upstream goal is to **bring together the best parts of Origin UI** with a stronger behavioral
foundation, so developers get a layered system of **primitives**, **particles**, and **atoms**. COSS
for Svelte preserves that design while using Shards UI for the component behavior.

## Origin UI

Origin UI offered more than 600 ready-to-use UI components. With the **transition to COSS UI**:

- Existing **Origin UI components** evolve into **particles**, higher-level interface patterns
  composed from COSS primitives.
- The original Origin UI collection remains available on the upstream
  [Origin UI page](https://coss.com/origin) during the transition.

This repository does not copy or redistribute the legacy Origin UI source. Its permitted source
boundary is the MIT-designated COSS `reference/apps/ui` subtree.

## Current Status

- The upstream COSS project has more than 50 UI primitives, with more on the way.
- Its particle catalog continues to expand toward the breadth of the Origin UI collection.
- COSS for Svelte ports the primitives, particles, documentation, and registry items from the pinned
  COSS reference one lane at a time.
- Each Svelte implementation requires separate parity/accessibility and Svelte-quality review before
  integration.

## What's Next

- Complete the Svelte counterparts for the pinned primitive and particle catalog.
- Compare light, dark, desktop, mobile, pointer, keyboard, focus, and reduced-motion states against
  the COSS reference.
- Finish the shadcn-svelte registry installation workflow and agent-readable Markdown documentation.
- Let the upstream project continue expanding **particles** and introducing **atoms**, API-enhanced
  components with external integrations.

Atoms and the legacy Origin application are not part of the first COSS for Svelte release.
